{"step_index":48,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T02:34:15Z","content":"Two issues: MoviePy v2 changed imports, and the segmentation grabbed almost everything as one blob. Let me fix both — I'll use a **cartoon-optimized edge detection** approach (since this image has clear drawn outlines) and fix the MoviePy import.","thinking":"MoviePy v2.x changed the import path. Let me fix the import and also address the fact that only 2 objects were detected (the color segmentation needs tuning for this specific image). Let me fix both issues.\n\nThe main problems:\n1. `moviepy.editor` is deprecated in MoviePy 2.x — need to use `from moviepy import ...`\n2. Only 2 objects detected — need to improve the segmentation. The image has multiple distinct blobs but the HSV ranges I chose are probably too narrow or overlapping with the background.\n\nLet me fix the import first and see the result, then improve segmentation if needed.\n\nActually, let me look at what was detected:\nLooking at the results, one \"object\" is essentially the entire image frame while the other is just a tiny blob, which means my HSV ranges are too broad and picking up the wooden background along with everything else. I need a different segmentation strategy, likely edge-based detection instead of pure color thresholding for this cartoon-style image.\n\nThese would form cleaner masks for a cartoon-style image. Simpler still: since the background is a consistent textured wood tone and the blobs/character are distinctly colored, I could just isolate the non-background regions directly by contrast rather than relying on edge detection alone.\n\nNow I realize my earlier dark-color mask was inadvertently catching the shadow lines in the wood planks, merging everything into one connected blob, and the other color masks were too permissive too. I need to fix the MoviePy import for v2.x and then redo the segmentation logic to properly separate the wood background from the actual objects.\n\nWriting the updated script...","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"\"\"\nImage Object Popup Animation → Video with Sound Effects (v2)\n=============================================================\nOptimized for cartoon/illustration images with clear outlines.\nUses edge detection + flood fill to find individual objects.\nCompatible with MoviePy v2.x\n\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport cv2\nimport numpy as np\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom pydub import AudioSegment\nfrom pydub.generators import Sine\n\n# ============================================================\n# STEP 1: Generate synthetic pop SFX\n# ============================================================\ndef generate_pop_sound(output_path, freq_start=900, freq_end=350, duration_ms=80):\n    \"\"\"Creates a pitch-dropping 'pop' sound.\"\"\"\n    part1 = Sine(freq_start).to_audio_segment(duration=duration_ms // 2).fade_out(duration_ms // 3)\n    part2 = Sine(freq_end).to_audio_segment(duration=duration_ms // 2).fade_out(duration_ms // 3)\n    pop = part1 + part2\n    pop = pop - 5\n    pop.export(output_path, format=\"wav\")\n    return output_path\n\n\ndef generate_whoosh_sound(output_path, duration_ms=150):\n    \"\"\"Creates a quick whoosh/sweep sound.\"\"\"\n    sweep = AudioSegment.silent(duration=0)\n    steps = 15\n    for i in range(steps):\n        freq = 200 + (i * 80)\n        chunk = Sine(freq).to_audio_segment(duration=duration_ms // steps)\n        chunk = chunk - (3 + i * 0.5)\n        sweep += chunk\n    sweep = sweep.fade_in(30).fade_out(60)\n    sweep.export(output_path, format=\"wav\")\n    return output_path\n\n\n# ============================================================\n# STEP 2: Segment cartoon objects using EDGE + FLOOD FILL\n# ============================================================\ndef segment_cartoon_objects(image_path, min_area=2000, max_objects=12):\n    \"\"\"\n    Cartoon-optimized segmentation:\n    1. Detect black outlines via edge detection\n    2. Create a closed-edge map (dilate to seal gaps)  \n    3. Flood-fill each enclosed region\n    4. Filter by size to get meaningful objects\n    \"\"\"\n    img_bgr = cv2.imread(image_path)\n    if img_bgr is None:\n        print(f\"ERROR: Cannot load {image_path}\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n    \n    img_rgb = cv2.cvtColor(img_bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    h, w = img_bgr.shape[:2]\n    gray = cv2.cvtColor(img_bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n    \n    print(f\"  Image size: {w}x{h}\")\n    \n    # ---- METHOD: Color-based region segmentation ----\n    # Convert to HSV\n    hsv = cv2.cvtColor(img_bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2HSV)\n    \n    # The wooden background has specific color properties:\n    # It's olive/brown: H≈20-50, S≈30-120, V≈80-180\n    # We'll create a background mask and invert it\n    \n    # Background mask (wooden planks - olive/greenish-brown)\n    lower_bg1 = np.array([18, 20, 70])\n    upper_bg1 = np.array([50, 150, 200])\n    bg_mask = cv2.inRange(hsv, lower_bg1, upper_bg1)\n    \n    # Also include the dark line textures of the wood\n    lower_dark_bg = np.array([0, 0, 30])\n    upper_dark_bg = np.array([60, 100, 90])\n    bg_dark = cv2.inRange(hsv, lower_dark_bg, upper_dark_bg)\n    \n    # Combine background masks\n    bg_combined = cv2.bitwise_or(bg_mask, bg_dark)\n    \n    # Foreground = NOT background\n    fg_mask = cv2.bitwise_not(bg_combined)\n    \n    # Clean up with morphology\n    kernel_small = cv2.getStructuringElement(cv2.MORPH_ELLIPSE, (3, 3))\n    kernel_med = cv2.getStructuringElement(cv2.MORPH_ELLIPSE, (5, 5))\n    kernel_big = cv2.getStructuringElement(cv2.MORPH_ELLIPSE, (9, 9))\n    \n    # Remove tiny noise\n    fg_mask = cv2.morphologyEx(fg_mask, cv2.MORPH_OPEN, kernel_small, iterations=2)\n    # Close small holes within objects\n    fg_mask = cv2.morphologyEx(fg_mask, cv2.MORPH_CLOSE, kernel_med, iterations=3)\n    \n    # ---- Also detect edges to help separate touching objects ----\n    edges = cv2.Canny(gray, 50, 150)\n    # Thicken edges slightly\n    edges_thick = cv2.dilate(edges, kernel_small, iterations=1)\n    \n    # Use edges as separators: remove edge pixels from foreground\n    # This helps split objects that are close together\n    fg_separated = fg_mask.copy()\n    # Only subtract edges between large blobs, not within them\n    # Use a watershed-like approach instead:\n    \n    # Find distance transform to find centers of objects\n    dist_transform = cv2.distanceTransform(fg_mask, cv2.DIST_L2, 5)\n    _, sure_fg = cv2.threshold(dist_transform, 0.15 * dist_transform.max(), 255, 0)\n    sure_fg = np.uint8(sure_fg)\n    \n    # Find connected components on the cleaned foreground\n    num_labels, labels, stats, centroids = cv2.connectedComponentsWithStats(\n        fg_mask, connectivity=8\n    )\n    \n    print(f\"  Raw connected components: {num_labels - 1}\")  # -1 for background\n    \n    # Filter components by area\n    pil_img = Image.open(image_path).convert(\"RGBA\")\n    objects = []\n    \n    for label_id in range(1, num_labels):  # Skip background (0)\n        area = stats[label_id, cv2.CC_STAT_AREA]\n        \n        if area < min_area:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if this component is too large (>60% of image = it's the background)\n        if area > 0.6 * (w * h):\n            print(f\"  Skipping component {label_id} (too large: {area} px, likely background)\")\n            continue\n        \n        # Get bounding box\n        x = stats[label_id, cv2.CC_STAT_LEFT]\n        y = stats[label_id, cv2.CC_STAT_TOP]\n        bw = stats[label_id, cv2.CC_STAT_WIDTH]\n        bh = stats[label_id, cv2.CC_STAT_HEIGHT]\n        \n        # Get centroid\n        cx, cy = int(centroids[label_id][0]), int(centroids[label_id][1])\n        \n        # Create mask for this component\n        comp_mask = np.uint8(labels == label_id) * 255\n        \n        # Dilate slightly to include edges/outlines\n        comp_mask = cv2.dilate(comp_mask, kernel_small, iterations=2)\n        \n        # Feather edges\n        comp_mask_f = cv2.GaussianBlur(comp_mask.astype(np.float32), (7, 7), 2.0)\n        comp_mask_f = np.clip(comp_mask_f, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n        \n        # Add padding to bounding box\n        pad = 15\n        x1 = max(0, x - pad)\n        y1 = max(0, y - pad)\n        x2 = min(w, x + bw + pad)\n        y2 = min(h, y + bh + pad)\n        \n        # Extract RGBA cutout\n        img_array = np.array(pil_img)\n        cutout = img_array[y1:y2, x1:x2].copy()\n        mask_crop = comp_mask_f[y1:y2, x1:x2]\n        cutout[:, :, 3] = mask_crop\n        \n        cutout_pil = Image.fromarray(cutout)\n        \n        objects.append({\n            'image': cutout_pil,\n            'bbox': (x1, y1, x2, y2),\n            'centroid': (cx, cy),\n            'area': area,\n            'position': (x1, y1),\n            'index': len(objects)\n        })\n        \n        # Save cutout for inspection\n        cutout_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(image_path), f\"cutout_{len(objects)-1}.png\")\n        cutout_pil.save(cutout_path)\n        print(f\"  Object {len(objects)-1}: area={area}, center=({cx},{cy}), \"\n              f\"size={cutout_pil.size}, bbox=({x1},{y1})-({x2},{y2})\")\n    \n    if len(objects) > max_objects:\n        objects = objects[:max_objects]\n    \n    print(f\"  Total usable objects: {len(objects)}\")\n    \n    # ---- Create clean background via inpainting ----\n    all_obj_mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=np.uint8)\n    for label_id in range(1, num_labels):\n        area = stats[label_id, cv2.CC_STAT_AREA]\n        if area >= min_area and area <= 0.6 * (w * h):\n            mask = np.uint8(labels == label_id) * 255\n            all_obj_mask = cv2.bitwise_or(all_obj_mask, mask)\n    \n    # Dilate the combined mask\n    all_obj_mask = cv2.dilate(all_obj_mask, kernel_big, iterations=2)\n    \n    # Inpaint\n    bg_inpainted = cv2.inpaint(img_bgr, all_obj_mask, inpaintRadius=20, flags=cv2.INPAINT_TELEA)\n    bg_rgb = cv2.cvtColor(bg_inpainted, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    background = Image.fromarray(bg_rgb)\n    \n    bg_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(image_path), \"clean_background.png\")\n    background.save(bg_path)\n    print(f\"  Background saved: {bg_path}\")\n    \n    # Also save the foreground mask for debugging\n    mask_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(image_path), \"debug_fg_mask.png\")\n    cv2.imwrite(mask_path, fg_mask)\n    print(f\"  Debug mask saved: {mask_path}\")\n    \n    return background, objects\n\n\n# ============================================================\n# STEP 3: Easing functions\n# ============================================================\ndef ease_out_back(t, s=1.70158):\n    \"\"\"Overshoots to ~115% then settles — classic cartoon pop.\"\"\"\n    t = max(0.0, min(1.0, t))\n    t -= 1.0\n    return t * t * ((s + 1) * t + s) + 1.0\n\n\ndef ease_out_bounce(t):\n    \"\"\"Bouncy landing effect.\"\"\"\n    t = max(0.0, min(1.0, t))\n    if t < 1 / 2.75:\n        return 7.5625 * t * t\n    elif t < 2 / 2.75:\n        t -= 1.5 / 2.75\n        return 7.5625 * t * t + 0.75\n    elif t < 2.5 / 2.75:\n        t -= 2.25 / 2.75\n        return 7.5625 * t * t + 0.9375\n    else:\n        t -= 2.625 / 2.75\n        return 7.5625 * t * t + 0.984375\n\n\n# ============================================================\n# STEP 4: Build the popup animation video with SFX\n# ============================================================\ndef build_popup_video(\n    image_path,\n    output_path=\"popup_animation.mp4\",\n    pop_duration=0.5,\n    interval=0.7,\n    initial_delay=0.3,\n    trailing_pause=2.0,\n    fps=30,\n    easing=\"back\"\n):\n    print(\"=\" * 60)\n    print(\"  IMAGE POPUP VIDEO GENERATOR v2\")\n    print(\"=\" * 60)\n    \n    # --- Segment ---\n    print(\"\\n[1/4] Segmenting objects...\")\n    background, objects = segment_cartoon_objects(image_path)\n    \n    if not objects:\n        print(\"ERROR: No objects found!\")\n        return\n    \n    w_bg, h_bg = background.size\n    num_objects = len(objects)\n    \n    # Sort: smallest objects first, biggest (main character) last for dramatic reveal\n    objects.sort(key=lambda o: o['area'])\n    \n    total_duration = initial_delay + (num_objects * interval) + trailing_pause\n    \n    print(f\"\\n[2/4] Building animation timeline...\")\n    print(f\"  Objects: {num_objects}\")\n    print(f\"  Duration: {total_duration:.1f}s\")\n    \n    ease_fn = ease_out_back if easing == \"back\" else ease_out_bounce\n    \n    # --- Generate sounds ---\n    print(\"\\n[3/4] Generating sound effects...\")\n    project_dir = os.path.dirname(image_path) or \".\"\n    pop_sfx_path = os.path.join(project_dir, \"sfx_pop.wav\")\n    whoosh_sfx_path = os.path.join(project_dir, \"sfx_whoosh.wav\")\n    generate_pop_sound(pop_sfx_path)\n    generate_whoosh_sound(whoosh_sfx_path)\n    \n    pop_sfx = AudioSegment.from_file(pop_sfx_path)\n    whoosh_sfx = AudioSegment.from_file(whoosh_sfx_path)\n    \n    master_audio = AudioSegment.silent(duration=int(total_duration * 1000))\n    \n    # --- Render frames ---\n    print(\"\\n[4/4] Rendering frames...\")\n    total_frames = int(total_duration * fps)\n    frames = []\n    \n    bg_array = np.array(background.convert(\"RGB\"))\n    \n    for frame_idx in range(total_frames):\n        t = frame_idx / fps\n        frame = Image.fromarray(bg_array.copy())\n        \n        for obj_idx, obj in enumerate(objects):\n            appear_time = initial_delay + obj_idx * interval\n            \n            if t < appear_time:\n                continue\n            \n            local_t = t - appear_time\n            progress = min(1.0, local_t / pop_duration)\n            scale = ease_fn(progress)\n            \n            if scale < 0.01:\n                continue\n            \n            obj_img = obj['image']\n            obj_w, obj_h = obj_img.size\n            \n            new_w = max(1, int(obj_w * scale))\n            new_h = max(1, int(obj_h * scale))\n            \n            scaled_img = obj_img.resize((new_w, new_h), Image.LANCZOS)\n            \n            # Center the scaled image on the original position\n            pos_x = obj['position'][0] + (obj_w - new_w) // 2\n            pos_y = obj['position'][1] + (obj_h - new_h) // 2\n            \n            frame.paste(scaled_img, (pos_x, pos_y), scaled_img)\n        \n        frames.append(np.array(frame))\n        \n        if frame_idx % fps == 0:\n            print(f\"  Frame {frame_idx}/{total_frames} ({t:.1f}s)\")\n    \n    # --- Place SFX ---\n    for obj_idx in range(num_objects):\n        appear_time = initial_delay + obj_idx * interval\n        appear_ms = int(appear_time * 1000)\n        sfx = pop_sfx if obj_idx % 2 == 0 else whoosh_sfx\n        master_audio = master_audio.overlay(sfx, position=appear_ms)\n    \n    audio_path = os.path.join(project_dir, \"master_audio.wav\")\n    master_audio.export(audio_path, format=\"wav\")\n    \n    # --- Encode video (MoviePy v2 imports) ---\n    print(\"\\n  Encoding video with MoviePy v2...\")\n    try:\n        # MoviePy v2.x\n        from moviepy import ImageSequenceClip, AudioFileClip\n    except ImportError:\n        # MoviePy v1.x fallback\n        from moviepy.editor import ImageSequenceClip, AudioFileClip\n    \n    video_clip = ImageSequenceClip(frames, fps=fps)\n    audio_clip = AudioFileClip(audio_path)\n    video_clip = video_clip.with_audio(audio_clip)\n    \n    video_clip.write_videofile(\n        output_path,\n        fps=fps,\n        codec=\"libx264\",\n        audio_codec=\"aac\",\n        logger=\"bar\"\n    )\n    \n    # Cleanup\n    for f in [audio_path, pop_sfx_path, whoosh_sfx_path]:\n        if os.path.exists(f):\n            os.remove(f)\n    \n    print(f\"\\n{'=' * 60}\")\n    print(f\"  ✅ DONE! Video saved: {output_path}\")\n    print(f\"{'=' * 60}\")\n\n\n# ============================================================\n# RUN\n# ============================================================\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    script_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\n    input_image = os.path.join(script_dir, \"input_image.jpg\")\n    output_video = os.path.join(script_dir, \"popup_animation.mp4\")\n    \n    build_popup_video(\n        image_path=input_image,\n        output_path=output_video,\n        pop_duration=0.5,\n        interval=0.7,\n        initial_delay=0.3,\n        trailing_pause=2.0,\n        fps=30,\n        easing=\"back\"\n    )\n","Description":"Fixed MoviePy v2 import (moviepy.editor → moviepy), rewrote segmentation to use connected components with background color exclusion instead of broad foreground detection that merged everything into one blob.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\popup-project\\popup_video.py","toolAction":"Rewriting fixed script","toolSummary":"Fixed pipeline script"}}]}
